This Python script uses a Genetic Algorithm to solve the Graph Coloring Problem, aiming to color a network of nodes so that no connected nodes share the same color.
Here is the code's execution flow in short:

* Initialization: It sets up an initial population of random color configurations based on user inputs (nodes, connections, and color limits).
* Selection & Crossover: In each generation, it sorts the population to find the best layouts. It picks two parents from the top 5 configurations and blends their colors 50/50 to create a child layout.
* Targeted Mutation: If the new child layout has connected nodes with matching colors, there is a 30% chance the algorithm will step in and assign a brand-new random color to one of those conflicting nodes.
* Output: It preserves the best-performing layout into the next generation. It stops early if it achieves a perfect, conflict-free layout and displays a visual map of the network using NetworkX and Matplotlib.


